counterfactuals.output:
A bearer management device may reliably process an incoming packet-switched call during relocation of a mobile terminal between location-management nodes by temporarily retaining the call request until the relocation is completed and thereafter directing the request to the serving location-management node.

In particular, a method performed by the bearer management device may comprise:

1. receiving an incoming packet-call establishment request addressed to a mobile terminal;
2. determining, from mobility-management signaling or subscriber-location information, that relocation of the mobile terminal from a first location-management node to a second location-management node is in progress;
3. storing the call-establishment request and associating the request with a transaction identifier of the mobile terminal;
4. inhibiting final routing or rejection of the incoming packet call while the relocation remains pending;
5. obtaining an indication that the relocation has been completed and identifying the second location-management node as the serving node;
6. forwarding, or re-originating, the stored call-establishment request toward the second location-management node; and
7. removing the stored request upon receipt of an acknowledgement, call-establishment response, or terminal failure indication.

The bearer management device may further start a timer upon detecting the relocation condition. If completion of the relocation is not indicated before expiration of the timer, the device may query a subscriber-location register, route the request toward the most recently confirmed serving node, or release the call with a prescribed cause value. Duplicate requests may be suppressed by comparing transaction identifiers, sequence information, or terminal identifiers.

Accordingly, the bearer management device comprises processing circuitry, memory, and communication interfaces configured to detect an ongoing location-management-node change, buffer an incoming packet-call request during the change, resolve the serving location-management node following the change, and deliver the buffered request to the resolved node, thereby preventing loss, misrouting, or duplicate establishment of the incoming packet call.
